#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074893} :activity/id #uuid "48334486-8ae7-5572-8e8f-b176d6318f35" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050138 :topic/id #uuid "2fda426e-c98b-5b36-800a-304f9ce90e0d" :topic/math-academy-id 554} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075038 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n![](images/6c/6cda61e0fbd0d9695644a29f0c6fa3478eae84cf253623da7f4b544f35873472.svg)\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10},$ which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 2}{10\\times 2} & =\\frac{10}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca7b3d51-7281-5705-bed5-276f34939c25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8357 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ac8583b6-39eb-5a51-9b51-c3c4ebec13fa" :step/math-academy-id 81656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074894}}